[Event "Winning with the Caro-Kann: What is the Caro-Kann?"]
[Date "2026.07.12"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense"]
[StudyName "Winning with the Caro-Kann"]
[ChapterName "What is the Caro-Kann?"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/i5EqteMO/ssTBJyDn"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/DuoCalc31123"]
[UTCDate "2026.07.12"]
[UTCTime "21:12:21"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ One of the most solid chess openings is the Caro-Kann, 1.e4 c6. No matter what White's next move is, Black will play d5 to strike at the center. Unlike the French defense, the Caro-Kann does not block in the light-squared bishop. The Caro-Kann opening also gives the player with the Black pieces a favorable pawn structure. }
1. e4 { The Caro-Kann can only be played after 1.e4. After 1.e4, Black plays 1...c6. } 1... c6 2. d4 { 2.d4, taking the center, is the most popular move, played 42% of the time. How can you attack 1.e4? } 2... d5 { Correct! This is the Caro-Kann Defense. } *

[Event "Winning with the Caro-Kann: Caro-Kann Exchange Variation"]
