{ The Botvinnik-Carls Defense is a response to 3.e5 with the pawn break 3...c5, followed by 4...e3. The downside of this variation is that 4...e3 blocks in the light-squared bishop. For this reason, the Botvinnik-Carls Defense is generally considered worse than the Caro-Kann: Advance Main-Line (3...Bf4) from the last chapter. }
1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { Kick off the Botvinnik-Carls Defense with a pawn break! } 3... c5 4. c3 { Over half the time, White defends the d-pawn with 4.c3. What is the best continuation? } 4... Nc6 5. Nf3 cxd4 6. cxd4 Bg4 { Black has already equalized on move 6! If you play this rare line correctly, many opponents will eventually make mistakes. } *

